# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What never enters the build context.
#
# The first entry is the important one. `apps/purchasing/.data/` is the
# developer's SQLite database, and Next's standalone build copies the
# application directory wholesale — so without this line the image ships with a
# real pilot database inside it, and that is the database the container opens.
# The company's purchasing system would come up holding somebody else's records
# and lose the real ones on the next redeploy, with nothing in any log to say
# so. scripts/check-deployable.mjs is the second line of defence; this is the
# first.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Databases, journals, and anything that looks like one.
**/.data/
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3

# Secrets. The image takes configuration at RUNTIME, never baked in.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
*.pem
*.p12
id_rsa*

# Build outputs and dependencies — the image builds its own.
node_modules
**/node_modules
.next
**/.next
dist
**/dist
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Version control and CI.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Local tooling and editor noise.
.DS_Store
.vscode
.idea
.mcp.json
*.log

# Things that are not the application: the other apps in this monorepo, the
# Supabase stack, fixtures, and the documentation. The purchasing app and the
# workspace packages it imports are all the runtime needs.
apps/mobile
apps/web
supabase
fixtures
docs
PCC_*.md
SESSION_*.md

# Host service definitions. They configure the machine that RUNS the container;
# shipping them inside it would be a systemd unit describing its own cage.
deploy
